# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository and checked out as LF on
# every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with the default `core.autocrlf=true` gets CRLF
# on disk, which breaks the byte-pinned golden fixtures — `roam-golden.test.ts`
# and `book-head.test.ts` compare exact bytes and fail on a fresh Windows
# checkout for reasons that have nothing to do with the code under test.
#
# It also matters beyond tests: vault content is plain Markdown that must stay
# byte-identical across macOS / Windows / Linux (CLAUDE.md, "file-over-app"),
# and a mixed-ending repo is the usual way that guarantee starts to rot.
#
# Existing clones need `git add --renormalize .` once for this to take effect
# on files already in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.epub binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.notemdpkg binary

# Windows-only scripts want CRLF to stay runnable when opened by cmd.exe.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# One deliberate exception to the eol=lf rule above: this fixture exists to
# carry CRLF bytes into the CRLF-normalization test. Normalizing it to LF
# would make it a byte-identical copy of basic.note.md — the test would still
# pass while verifying nothing.
searchidx/tests/fixtures/outline/crlf.note.md -text

# Same exception, same reason: the task-11 acceptance corpus's own CRLF
# fixture (`crlfmarker` in tests/fixtures/retrievability.json) must survive
# git's clean/smudge filters untouched, or a fresh checkout silently turns it
# into a plain LF file and the "CRLF files index correctly" acceptance case
# stops testing anything.
searchidx/tests/fixtures/corpus/prose/crlf.md -text
